\label{First homology for w,h ge 3}
For integers $w,h\ge3$,
\[
H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big)\cong \begin{cases}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}& 2\le n\le wh-3,\\
\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{(w-1)(h-1)+1}&n=wh-2,\\
\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{(w-1)(h-1)}&n=wh-1,\\
0&\mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

\label{first homology of height 2 box}

For an integer $w\ge h=2$,
\[
H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,2})\big)\cong \begin{cases}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{n-1}&\mbox{if }1\le n\le2w-2,\\
\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{w-1}&\mbox{if }n=2w-1, \mbox{and}\\
0&\mbox{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

\label{V sets for slide ss}
If $0\le p<h$ and $1\le l\le p+1$, let $V_{l}$ be the union of the following two subspaces of $SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1; p)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item The subspace of configurations in which, after sliding  the squares labeled $3$ up and down, the square labeled $2$ can be slid directly to the right so that it is horizontally aligned with the squares labeled $3$ in a way so that of the squares labeled $2$ and $3$, it is in the $l$-th position when read from top to bottom.
\item The subspace of configurations in which the center of the square labeled $2$ straddles two rows, such that after sliding this square and the other squares that are directly above or below it up or down to be vertically aligned with either of the two rows of grid squares that partially contain it, the squares labeled $3$ can be slid up or down so that the square labeled $2$ can be slid to the right to be horizontally aligned with the squares labeled $3$ so that it is in the $l$-th position when read from top to bottom.
\end{itemize}

The sets $V_{l}$ cover $SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1; p)$, and we call the resulting augmented Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence for $SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1;p)$ the \emph{augmented slide spectral sequence}.
We write $\tilde{E}^{r}_{s,t}\big[SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1; p)\big]$ for the $(s,t)$-entry on the $r$-th page of this spectral sequence.

An \emph{orbiting pair square class} $\sigma\in H_{1}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big)$ is the class depicted on the left in Figure~\ref{Orbiting Square Classes}.
An \emph{orbiting triple square class} $\theta\in H_{1}\big(SC_{3}(R_{2,2})\big)$ is the class depicted on the right in Figure~\ref{Orbiting Square Classes}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=10cm]{Orbitingsqaureclasses.pdf}
\caption{On the left, an orbiting pair square class. 
On the right, an orbiting triple square class.
}
\label{Orbiting Square Classes}
\end{figure}

Embedding $R_{2,2}$ into $R_{w,h}$ yields classes in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};s; p)\big)$ that we also call
\emph{orbiting pair} and \emph{triple square classes}. 
Inside the embedded $R_{2,2}$ two or three squares orbit each other.
The remaining $n-2$ or $n-3$ squares are fixed outside of the embedded $R_{2,2}$ and are called \emph{singletons}. 
See Figure~\ref{Embedded Orbiting Pair Class} for an example.
Additionally, in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,2};s; p)\big)$, we say that an orbiting pair (triple) square class is \emph{even} if there is an even number of singleton squares to the left of the orbiting pair (triple).

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=5cm]{EmbeddedOrbitingPairClass.pdf}
\caption{An orbiting pair square class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{6}(R_{3,3})\big)$.
The class arises from embedding $R_{2,2}$ at the top left of $R_{3,3}$, which we have outlined in red.
There are four singleton squares.
}
\label{Embedded Orbiting Pair Class}
\end{figure}

In the $SC_{2}(R_{2,2})$ setting, note that $E^{1}_{1,0}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]=H_{0}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2};2)\big)\cong \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$, and it is generated by the class which can be represented by the $0$-chain on the left of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1}.
The image of this class under the $d^{1}$-differential is the difference of the two classes depicted on the right of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1}.
As these classes are homologous and $H_{0}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2};3)\big)$ is trivial by definition, we have that $E^{2}_{1,0}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]\cong \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=7.5cm]{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1.pdf}
\caption{On the left, a chain representing the generator of $E^{1}_{1,0}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]=H_{0}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2};2)\big)$.
On the right, the two chains in $E^{1}_{0,0}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]=H_{0}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2};1)\big)$ whose difference is its image under the $d^{1}$-differential.
They are homologous.
We have colored the squares labeled $1$ blue and the squares labeled $2$ yellow.}
\label{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1}
\end{figure}

The two classes on the right of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1} are the boundary of the $1$-chain depicted on the left of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential2}, and so the image of this class in $E^{2}_{-1,1}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]$ under $d^{1}$ is the class depicted on the right of the figure.
That class is an orbiting pair square class, and our argument shows that it is the image of the generator of $E^{2}_{1,0}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]$ under the $d^{2}$-differential as desired.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=3.75cm]{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential2.pdf}
\caption{On the left, the $1$-chain whose boundary is the difference of the two classes depicted on the right of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1}.
On the right, the image of this chain in $E^{2}_{-1,1}\big[SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big]=H_{1}\big(SC_{2}(R_{2,2})\big)$ under the $d^{1}$-differential.
It follows that this orbiting pair square class is the image of the class at the left of Figure~\ref{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential1} under the $d^{2}$-differential.}
\label{Orbitingpairsquaredifferential2}
\end{figure}

The analogous argument also works in the $SC_{3}(R_{2,2})$ setting with an extra square labeled $1$ on the left side of $R_{2,2}$.

If $w\ge 2$ and $2w-1\le n\le 2w$, the components correspond to the square labeled $2$ being above or below the square labeled $3$; see the top of Figure~\ref{ComponentsofSCnRw211}.
If $2\le n\le 2w-2$, then we can slide the squares labeled $2$ and $3$ into the two rightmost columns of $R_{w,2}$ and the squares labeled $1$ out of these two columns; see the bottom of Figure~\ref{ComponentsofSCnRw211}.
This space is connected.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=6.25cm]{ComponentsofSCnRw211.pdf}
\caption{At the top, the two components of $SC_{7}(R_{4,2};1;1)$.
Note that in the left configuration there is no way of moving the yellow square (the square labeled $2$) below the red square (the square labeled $3$), while not letting any of the blue squares (the squares labeled $1$) pass to the right of it, so as to move to the configuration on the right.
At the bottom, a configuration in the sole component of $SC_{6}(R_{4,2};1;1)$.
In this component the yellow square can be slid above or below the red square.}
\label{ComponentsofSCnRw211}
\end{figure}

In $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$, after sliding the square labeled $3$ to the far right of $R_{w,2}$, we may slide the square labeled $2$ to the right so that it is in one of the $2$ rightmost columns of $R_{w,2}$.
It follows from the definition of $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$ that in $V_{1}\cap V_{2}$ the square labeled $2$ must be in the second rightmost column moving between the top and bottom grid-squares; see Figure~\ref{V1intersectV2}.
Thus, no square labeled $1$ can be in the two rightmost columns of $R_{w,2}$.
Therefore, if we remove the two rightmost columns of $R_{w,2}$ and the squares labeled $2$ and $3$, we get $V_{1}\cap V_{2}\simeq SC_{n-2}(R_{w-2, 2})$.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=2.5cm]{V1intersectV2.pdf}
\caption{A configuration in $V_{1}\cap V_{2}$, where $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$ are the sets used to define the slide spectral sequence for $SC_{5}(R_{4,2};1;1)$.
Note that the square labeled $2$, which we color yellow, must be in between the top and the bottom grid squares in the second rightmost column.
This follows from the fact that after sliding that square up or down it must be able to be slid into the rightmost column of $R_{w,2}$ so as to be either above or below the square labeled $3$, which we have colored red.}
\label{V1intersectV2}
\end{figure}

Alvarado-Gardu\~{n}o, Gonz\'{a}lez, and Kahle proved that $SC_{2w-1}(R_{w,2})\simeq DC_{2w-1}(R_{w-1,1})$ is homotopy equivalent to the ladder graph $\Gamma_{w}$ on $2w$ vertices, which is homotopy equivalent to a wedge of $w-1$ circles \cite[Theorem 1.2]{MattOmarJesus}; see Figure~\ref{Gamma42}.
It follows that $H_{1}\big(SC_{2w-1}(R_{w,2})\big)\cong \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{w-1}$.
Moreover, the $w-1$ square loops in $\Gamma_{w}$ are the $w-1$ orbiting triple square classes coming from the $w-1$ grid-preserving embeddings of $R_{2,2}$ in $R_{w,2}$.
