\label{connected unordered square configuration spaces two rightmost}
Assume $w,h\ge 3$, $n\le wh-3$, $0< s+1\le 3$, and $0<p\le h$.
The square configuration space $SC_{n}(R_{w,h};s+1; p)$ is connected in each of the following cases:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)] $s+p+1 > h$;

  \item[(ii)] $s+p+1 \le h$ and $n \le (w-1)h+p-1$;

  \item[(iii)] $s+p+1 < h$ and $n = (w-1)h+p$.
\end{enumerate}
Otherwise, $SC_n(R_{w,h};s+1;p)$ has $\binom{s+p+1}{p}$ components.

\label{E201 entry of augmeneted right Mayer Vietoris ss}
Assume $w,h\ge 3$, $0\le p\le h$, and $n\le wh-3$.
If $n=p+2$, then $E^{2}_{1,0}\big[SC_{n}(R_{w,h};p)\big]\cong \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$; otherwise, $E^{2}_{1,0}\big[SC_{n}(R_{w,h};p)\big]=0$.

\label{H1 SC(1p1)}
Given  $w,h\ge 2$, $0<p<h$, and $n\le wh-3$, set
\[
\epsilon:=\begin{cases}
    p(h-p-2)&\text{if }h\ge 4\text{ and }n=(w-1)h+p, \text{ and}\\0&\text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
Then there is a natural short exact sequence 
\[
0\to Q\to H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};1;p)\big)\to \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}^{\epsilon}\to0,
\]
where $Q$ is a quotient of $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};p+1)\big)^{\oplus (p+1)}$ and the free summand is generated by the exotic classes defined in Definition~\ref{H1 SC(1p1) for n-p-1=(w-1)h}.

\label{connected intersection of Vs n-p-1 < (w-1)h}
Let $V_{1},\dots, V_{p+1}$ be the subspaces of $SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1; p)$ used to define the augmented slide spectral sequence, and assume that $w,h\ge 2$ and $0\le p< h$.
Given $1\le s\le p$, fix $1\le l_{0}<\cdots<l_{s}\le p+1$ and write $V:=V_{l_{0}}\cap \cdots\cap V_{l_{s}}$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $n<(w-1)h+p$, then $V\neq \emptyset$ if and only if $l_{s}-l_{0}\le h-p-1$, and in that case $V$ is connected.

    \item If $n=(w-1)h+p$, then $V\neq \emptyset$ if and only if $l_{s}-l_{0}\le h-p-1$, and in that case $V$ has $h-p+l_{0}-l_{s}$ components.

    \item If $n>(w-1)h+p$, then $V=\emptyset$. 
\end{enumerate}

\label{nerve complex is contractible}
Let $\mathcal{V}:=\{V_{l}\}_{l=1}^{p+1}$ be the cover of $SC_{n}(R_{w,h};1;p)$ used to define the slide spectral sequence.
If $w,h\ge 2$, $0\le p< h$ and $n\neq (w-1)h+p$, then the nerve complex $N(\mathcal{V})$ of this cover is homotopy equivalent to a union of points.

\label{H1 SC(1p1) for n-p-1=(w-1)h}
Suppose $w\ge2$, $h\ge 4$, $0< p< h$ and $n=(w-1)h+p\le wh-3$.
An \emph{exotic class} in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};1;p)\big)$ is a class of the form depicted in Figure~\ref{exotic class}, where the other squares labeled $1$ or $3$ are fixed outside of the embedded $R_{2,4}$ in $R_{w,h}$.
These classes are a basis for the image of the $\tilde{E}^{2}_{1,0}$-entry of the slide spectral sequence of $SC_{n}(R_{w,h};1;p)$ in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};1;p)\big)$ under the $\tilde{d}^{2}$-differential.


\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=8cm]{ExoticClass.pdf}
\caption{An exotic class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{5}(R_{2,4}; 1; 1)\big)$ that arises from the $\tilde{E}^{2}_{1,0}\big[SC_{5}(R_{2,4}; 1; 1)\big]$-entry of the augmented slide spectral sequence.
}
\label{exotic class}
\end{figure}

Similarly, the image of an exotic class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1;p)\big)$ under the $d^{1}$-differential of the augmented right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence of $SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; p)$ is an \emph{exotic class} in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; p)\big)$; see Figure~\ref{exotic class single rightmost}.
By iterating the augmented slide and right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence, we get \emph{exotic classes} in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1;p)\big)$ and $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h};p)\big)$ for general $n$ and $p$.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=10cm]{ExoticClasssinglerightmost.pdf}
\caption{An exotic class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{9}(R_{3,4}; 1)\big)$ that arises from applying the $d^{1}$-differential of the augmented right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence to $E^{1}_{0,1}\big[SC_{9}(R_{3,4}; 1)\big]\cong H_{1}\big(SC_{9}(R_{3,4};1;1)\big)$.}
\label{exotic class single rightmost}
\end{figure}

\label{intermediate generated by orbiting square classes}
Assume $w,h\ge 3$, $1\le p+1\le h$, and $n\le wh-3$.
If $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; p+1)\big)$ is generated by orbiting pair square classes and exotic classes, then so is $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; p)\big)$.

Since there are no exotic classes when $h=3$, we focus on the case $h\ge 4$.

By the definition of the augmented right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence for $SC_{n}(R_{w,h})$, we have that $E^{1}_{0,1}\big[SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big]\cong H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1)\big)$.
Consider an exotic class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h}; 1)\big)$ as in Figure~\ref{exotic class single rightmost}.
We will see that the image of this class under the $d^{1}$-differential is trivial.

By definition, the $d^{1}$-differential of the augmented right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence for $SC_{n}(R_{w,h})$ on the $E^{1}_{0,1}\big[SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big]$-entry has the effect of changing the label of the square labeled $2$ to $1$, i.e., removing all labels.
As can be seen in Figure~\ref{trivial exotic class}, this makes an exotic class trivial in homology.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=10cm]{TrivalExoticClasss.pdf}
\caption{An exotic class in $H_{1}\big(SC_{9}(R_{3,4})\big)$ that arises from applying the $d^{1}$-differential of the augmented right Mayer--Vietoris spectral sequence to $E^{1}_{0,1}\big[SC_{9}(R_{3,4})\big]\cong H_{1}\big(SC_{9}(R_{3,4};1)\big)$; see Figure~\ref{exotic class single rightmost}.
Observe that the top, left, and bottom squares can be rearranged, so this class is trivial, as the two squares moving at the middle right do not wind about each other.}
\label{trivial exotic class}
\end{figure}

If $T$ denotes the $T$-shaped tetromino, an \emph{orbiting pair $T$ class} $\tau\in H_{1}\big(SC_{2}(T)\big)$ is the class depicted in Figure~\ref{Orbiting Pair Classes}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=5cm]{OrbitingPairTClass.pdf}
\caption{An orbiting pair $T$ class.
}
\label{Orbiting Pair Classes}
\end{figure}

Embedding $T$ into $R_{w,h}$ yields \emph{orbiting pair $T$ classes} in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big)$.
Inside the embedded $T$ two  squares orbit each other.
The remaining $n-2$ singleton squares are fixed outside of the embedded region.

\label{2 squares in P is homotopy equivalent to 2 squares in R22}
Let $P$ be the area 5 pentomino depicted in Figure~\ref{PPentomino}.
Then $SC_{2}(P)\simeq SC_{2}(R_{2,2})$.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=1.25cm]{PPentomino.pdf}
\caption{The "$P$" pentomino.
}
\label{PPentomino}
\end{figure}

\label{orbiting square class is homologous to orbiting t class}
Let $\sigma$ and $\tau$ denote the orbiting pair square and $T$ classes in $H_{1}\big(SC_{2}(P)\big)$ that arise from the natural embeddings of $R_{2,2}$ and $T$ in $P$.
Then $\sigma$ and $\tau$ are homologous in $H_{1}\big(SC_{2}(P)\big)$.

Let $\sigma_{1}$ and $\sigma_{2}$ be orbiting pair square classes in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big)$.
Since the singletons can be moved to any configuration around the orbiting pair, it suffices to show that if $\sigma_{1}$ and $\sigma_{2}$ are adjacent as in the top of Figure~\ref{Adjacent Orbiting Pair Classes}, then they are homologous.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\captionsetup{width=.8\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=16cm]{AdjacentOrbitingPairClassesEquivalence.pdf}
\caption{The equivalence in homology between two adjacent orbiting pair square classes $\sigma_{1}$ and $\sigma_{2}$.
The large diagonal arrows are given by Proposition~\ref{orbiting square class is homologous to orbiting t class}, and the large horizontal arrow is a homotopy equivalence between the resulting orbiting pair $T$ classes that arises by sliding the other squares about $R_{3,3}$ while avoiding the embedded $T$ tetromino.
}
\label{Adjacent Orbiting Pair Classes}
\end{figure}

Given two adjacent orbiting pair square classes $\sigma_{1}$ and $\sigma_{2}$ in $H_{1}\big(SC_{n}(R_{w,h})\big)$, note that by our assumptions on $n$, we know that $\sigma_{1}$ can be realized such that there is at least one unoccupied square in the embedded $R_{2,2}$ used to realize $\sigma_{2}$ not lying in the embedded $R_{2,2}$ used to realize $\sigma_{1}$.
By Proposition~\ref{orbiting square class is homologous to orbiting t class}, it follows that $\sigma_{1}$ is homologous to an orbiting pair $T$ class $\tau_{1}$ by extending the embedded $R_{2,2}$ to an embedded $P$ pentomino by using the unoccupied square in the $R_{2,2}$ corresponding to $\sigma_{2}$.
Since $w,h\ge 3$, the singleton squares can be slid around $R_{w,h}$ avoiding the embedded $T$ tetromino used to realize $\tau_{1}$ to get a homologous class $\tau_{2}$ in which none of the singleton squares are in the $R_{2,2}$ used to realize $\sigma_{2}$.
Again, applying Proposition~\ref{orbiting square class is homologous to orbiting t class}, we see that $\tau_{2}$ and $\sigma_{2}$ are homologous, completing the proof.
See Figure~\ref{Adjacent Orbiting Pair Classes} for a visualization of this equivalence.

The $\text{Arc}_{p}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)$ come together to form a semisimplicial complex $\text{Arc}_{\bullet}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)$, with simplicial differentials $d_{h}$ defined by taking the alternating sum of the face maps $d_{l}:\text{Arc}_{p}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)\to \text{Arc}_{p-1}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)$ that forget the $l$-th arc; see Figure~\ref{ArcResHorDiff}.
As $\text{Arc}_{-1}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)\simeq C_{n}(M)$, the complex $\text{Arc}_{\bullet}\big(C_{n}(M)\big)$ is the \emph{unordered arc resolution of $C_{n}(M)$}.
The resulting augmented geometric realization spectral sequence is the \emph{augmented arc resolution spectral sequence of $C_{n}(M)$}.
We write $E^{r}_{p,q}[M](n)$ for the augmented arc resolution spectral sequence of $C_{n}(M)$, and $E^{r}_{p,q}(n)$ if $M=\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^{2}$.
